{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>to kill me and my children and my cattle with thirst</b> — They mention even the cattle in their complaint, saying to him that they require much water, and he must find a solution for all of them. Therefore it is stated in the second instance, <b>and abundant water came out, and the congregation drank, and their cattle</b> (Numbers 20:11). And our Sages said (Mekhilta here) that they regarded their cattle like their own persons, for they said: an animal is a person's life; one who travels on the road, if his animal is not with him, he perishes. And the reason they mentioned \"me and my children\" and did not simply say \"to kill us with thirst\" or \"to kill all this congregation,\" which would include men, women, and children, as they said in other places (Exodus 16:3), is that they mentioned the children to increase the complaint against him so that he would hurry to speak, for the little ones cannot endure thirst at all and would die before their fathers' eyes, as in the sense of <b>the tongue of the suckling cleaves to his palate with thirst</b> (Lamentations 4:4)."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:09:35.393979141Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}